WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.811
At first thought of the skeletal system, you might think of a lot of

00:00:05.811 --> 00:00:08.391
bones with a lot of names to memorize.

00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:12.574
That’s fair. You might know bones consist of a protein known as collagen

00:00:12.574 --> 00:00:13.557
as well as minerals.

00:00:13.857 --> 00:00:18.041
But one thing many don’t realize is that bones are a LIVING tissue.

00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:22.281
And there are so many things going on in bones – no bones about it, this

00:00:22.281 --> 00:00:23.752
video is going to be interesting.

00:00:24.052 --> 00:00:26.825
Now our focus is going to be on the human skeletal system.

00:00:27.125 --> 00:00:30.408
This system includes bones and also connective tissue like: (1)

00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:33.777
ligaments - generally connecting bone to bone, (2) tendons – which

00:00:33.777 --> 00:00:37.296
generally connect muscle to bone – and also (3) cartilage which we’ll

00:00:37.296 --> 00:00:37.700
get to later.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.716
I also emphasize “human” to point out: there are different kinds of

00:00:41.716 --> 00:00:43.656
skeletal systems in different organisms.

00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:46.716
For example, in our animal video we mention earthworms.

00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:51.203
Earthworms have a hydrostatic skeleton- a skeleton that is filled with

00:00:51.203 --> 00:00:52.837
liquid and kept under pressure.

00:00:53.137 --> 00:00:55.557
We also mention insects in our animal video.

00:00:55.857 --> 00:00:59.578
Insects have an exoskeleton – a hard outer skeleton made of chitin.

00:00:59.878 --> 00:01:03.608
In order to grow, most insects have to molt which involves shedding

00:01:03.608 --> 00:01:04.569
their old exoskeleton.

00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:08.873
And then many other animals have a hard skeleton on the inside called an

00:01:08.873 --> 00:01:11.700
endoskeleton: humans, for example, have an endoskeleton.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.675
The skeletal system has an important job of supporting your body and

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:16.972
protecting the organs inside.

00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:21.000
But your skeletal system also provides storage of important minerals,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.682
involves the making of red and white blood cells, and ---along with the

00:01:24.682 --> 00:01:26.478
help of muscles of course ---- movement.

00:01:26.778 --> 00:01:29.240
A reminder that body systems don’t work in isolation.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.828
In fact, the term “musculoskeletal system” used in a lot of textbooks

00:01:32.828 --> 00:01:36.026
combines the skeletal system with the muscular system – highlighting

00:01:36.026 --> 00:01:36.700
their work together.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.057
An adult human generally has 206 bones --- a few more when they were a

00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:43.900
baby but some of the bones fuse together by adulthood.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.911
The human skeleton can be divided into the axial skeleton and the

00:01:47.911 --> 00:01:48.843
appendicular skeleton.

00:01:49.143 --> 00:01:53.000
The axial skeleton – think central axis of a human – includes the bones

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.769
in the skull, in your ears (those are called ossicles), bone in your

00:01:56.769 --> 00:02:00.135
throat (called the hyoid), vertebral column, and the ribcage.

00:02:00.435 --> 00:02:04.753
As you can imagine, muscles attach to bones in the axial skeleton to let

00:02:04.753 --> 00:02:08.469
you move parts of your body like your head and neck as well as breathe.

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:12.306
It also offers a foundation to the other division: the appendicular

00:02:12.306 --> 00:02:16.000
skeleton. All the bones of the arms and the shoulder girdle where they

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.401
attach are included – essential for the functioning of arms and hands-

00:02:19.401 --> 00:02:22.850
and all the bones of the legs and the pelvic girdle where they attach –

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:23.700
essential for movement.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.700
Let’s talk about bone shape because that can be used to classify bones.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.068
Long bones: You know, it’s not that they have to be “long” per se: it’s

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:36.023
more about their cylinder shape and the fact that they’re longer than

00:02:36.023 --> 00:02:36.570
they are wide.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.468
These bones tend to be moved in muscle contraction.

00:02:39.768 --> 00:02:42.562
The femur in the leg is a great example of a long bone.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.545
The tibia and fibula in the legs are also long bones.

00:02:45.845 --> 00:02:48.759
In the arms, you can have the humerus, ulna, and radius.

00:02:49.059 --> 00:02:53.294
And part of the hands, you have metacarpals and phalanges - and part of

00:02:53.294 --> 00:02:55.700
the feet, you have metatarsals and phalanges.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.453
Short bones: unlike long bones, these are more like a cube as their

00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:01.032
length and width are close to equal.

00:03:01.332 --> 00:03:04.988
These bones are also moved using muscle contraction and these bones can

00:03:04.988 --> 00:03:05.552
offer stability.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.700
Carpals in the wrists and tarsals of the ankles are examples.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.336
Sesamoid bones: like a sesame seed, they are roundish.

00:03:12.636 --> 00:03:14.915
They’re great about handling pressure.

00:03:15.215 --> 00:03:16.700
The patella is an example.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.906
Flat bones: so unfortunately you don’t want to just go on the name

00:03:20.906 --> 00:03:22.821
because they aren’t necessarily flat.

00:03:23.121 --> 00:03:24.104
They’re often curved.

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:28.601
And thin. Skull bones (aka cranial bones) and shoulder blade bones (aka

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:29.700
scapulae) are examples.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.295
And irregular bones: a shape that is…irregular.

00:03:32.595 --> 00:03:36.541
Basically there isn’t a specific shape description you can use to define

00:03:36.541 --> 00:03:40.024
them. Irregular bones can be protective from an assortment of forces.

00:03:40.324 --> 00:03:41.700
Vertebrae are a great example.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.700
Let’s talk a bit about what’s going on in the inside of bones.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.091
There are two general types of bone tissue.

00:03:47.391 --> 00:03:51.196
Compact bone tissue: an outer layer that is hard and then spongy bone

00:03:51.196 --> 00:03:53.559
tissue, a layer where you will find bone marrow.

00:03:53.859 --> 00:03:57.722
Some of it is yellow marrow and it stores fat, which is important for

00:03:57.722 --> 00:03:58.867
long term energy storage.

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:00.033
And then red marrow.

00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:02.144
That’s where blood cells can be made.

00:04:02.444 --> 00:04:06.105
Red blood cells – which transport gases - and white blood cells -which

00:04:06.105 --> 00:04:07.485
are important for defending the body.

00:04:07.785 --> 00:04:10.923
Red marrow also involves platelet production – platelets aren’t really

00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:14.094
cells but cellular fragments that are critical in allowing blood to

00:04:14.094 --> 00:04:17.844
clot. So overall, it makes sense that we say bones are vascular because

00:04:17.844 --> 00:04:19.158
they have a rich blood supply.

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:23.000
Interesting fact: in emergencies, there is even a way to administer

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.372
fluids and medications into the bone in a similar way they can be

00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:29.700
delivered by an IV.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.682
Let's talk about cells involved with bone or cartilage.

00:04:32.982 --> 00:04:33.747
First, osteoblasts.

00:04:34.047 --> 00:04:35.451
These are cells that make bone.

00:04:35.751 --> 00:04:39.682
They can mature into another type of cell called osteocytes; these cells

00:04:39.682 --> 00:04:43.473
maintain bone structure and make up the majority of bone cells present.

00:04:43.773 --> 00:04:47.864
Osteoclasts – these cell actually break down bone structure and the way

00:04:47.864 --> 00:04:51.245
they do it is cool: remember the organelles called lysosomes?

00:04:51.545 --> 00:04:53.852
Well, osteoclasts have a lot of lysosomes.

00:04:54.152 --> 00:04:58.021
In lysosomes, there are enzymes there and together with acids, they can

00:04:58.021 --> 00:04:58.762
help dissolve bone.

00:04:59.062 --> 00:05:02.200
You might wonder: why would you want to normally break down bone?

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.170
Well, the body is always responding to stresses and some parts of old

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:10.000
bone is removed and then some parts are rebuilt in what is called “bone

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.700
remodeling,” so osteoclasts are needed for this normal process.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.372
It’s estimated that every year, approximately 5-10% of a skeleton is

00:05:18.372 --> 00:05:23.233
remodeled. So remodeling in the skeletal system means bone that has been

00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:26.491
damaged or is just old is reabsorbed and replaced.

00:05:26.791 --> 00:05:30.670
If you get injured or if you do a lot of exercise, this can also impact

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:35.032
remodeling. In addition, the process of breaking down bone also releases

00:05:35.032 --> 00:05:36.689
minerals the body may require.

00:05:36.989 --> 00:05:38.861
A major mineral bones store is calcium.

00:05:39.161 --> 00:05:42.645
Specific hormones from the endocrine system actually can signal whether

00:05:42.645 --> 00:05:45.984
calcium in the bones may need to be released; specific hormones also

00:05:45.984 --> 00:05:47.453
signals when calcium is deposited.

00:05:47.753 --> 00:05:51.570
Calcium has a lot of functions for body processes; for example, in our

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:55.441
muscle contraction video, we show how important calcium is in order for

00:05:55.441 --> 00:05:56.700
muscle contraction to occur.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.421
Bone cells work alongside other cells.

00:05:59.721 --> 00:06:01.374
For example, chondroblasts.

00:06:01.674 --> 00:06:04.419
Chondroblasts make the connective tissue cartilage.

00:06:04.719 --> 00:06:08.000
Chondroblasts can mature into chondrocytes which maintain this

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.163
cartilage; they make up the cartilage.

00:06:10.463 --> 00:06:14.549
Cartilage supports bone; it’s important in joints where bones are

00:06:14.549 --> 00:06:17.157
connected. It also is a template for bone placement.

00:06:17.457 --> 00:06:21.409
In fetal development, it is cartilage – made by chondroblasts - that is

00:06:21.409 --> 00:06:23.700
meant to serve as the matrix for the bones.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.467
Eventually bone will replace most – but not all – of the cartilage

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:32.824
matrix. With the help of osteoblasts, bones grow in length usually until

00:06:32.824 --> 00:06:33.524
early adulthood.

00:06:33.824 --> 00:06:37.181
Bones can also grow in diameter, even after they stop growing in length.

00:06:37.481 --> 00:06:40.617
The process for bone growth is complex: it involves learning about

00:06:40.617 --> 00:06:42.657
different zones, including the growth plate zone.

00:06:42.957 --> 00:06:45.700
We have a suggested read in our video description to learn more.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.741
What happens when a bone breaks?

00:06:48.041 --> 00:06:52.237
So typically after a break, there is a fracture hematoma- which is a

00:06:52.237 --> 00:06:55.553
collection of blood that forms at the site of the break because again

00:06:55.553 --> 00:06:58.868
bones are very vascular- and because blood is blocked from getting to

00:06:58.868 --> 00:07:02.761
the bone cells that exist near the fracture, those bone cells generally

00:07:02.761 --> 00:07:07.000
die. But thanks to the help of chondrocytes and osteoblasts – an

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.869
internal callus and external callus is made there to keep it all

00:07:10.869 --> 00:07:15.095
together. The internal callus is mainly cartilage and the outer callus

00:07:15.095 --> 00:07:16.700
is a mix of cartilage and bone.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.313
So what’s going to remove the damaged part of the bone?

00:07:20.613 --> 00:07:21.603
Osteoclasts, right?

00:07:21.903 --> 00:07:26.324
And they do. And osteoblasts will get to work on the remodeling – which

00:07:26.324 --> 00:07:29.046
means – they ultimately will make new bone there.

00:07:29.346 --> 00:07:33.758
It takes time for this to occur though: many fractures heal in 6-8 weeks

00:07:33.758 --> 00:07:36.811
but it can be longer depending on an assortment of variables and the

00:07:36.811 --> 00:07:37.808
type of bone that was broken.

00:07:38.108 --> 00:07:41.178
It can also matter on the fracture: there are a lot of different kinds

00:07:41.178 --> 00:07:41.484
of fractures.

00:07:41.784 --> 00:07:45.861
If a fracture is from a severe trauma – let’s say to a long bone like

00:07:45.861 --> 00:07:49.616
the femur – not only can this cause excessive internal bleeding but

00:07:49.616 --> 00:07:52.976
remember how we mentioned that bone has yellow marrow which stores fat?

00:07:53.276 --> 00:07:57.256
This fat can also be released into the blood and while usually the body

00:07:57.256 --> 00:08:01.236
can clear this, it is possible – though rare- to develop what is called

00:08:01.236 --> 00:08:03.700
fat embolism syndrome which needs emergency care.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.091
While many often are familiar with fractures, there are also conditions

00:08:08.091 --> 00:08:10.348
that can affect bone development and growth.

00:08:10.648 --> 00:08:12.620
One example is osteogenesis imperfecta.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:17.000
There are multiple forms of this genetic disorder- but generally,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.806
collagen production is impacted.

00:08:19.106 --> 00:08:22.859
If you recall from the very beginning of our video, collagen is the

00:08:22.859 --> 00:08:23.776
major protein in bone.

00:08:24.076 --> 00:08:27.324
With this condition, bones tend to be brittle and easily fracture.

00:08:27.624 --> 00:08:31.468
While there is not a cure yet - treatment plans aim to help with

00:08:31.468 --> 00:08:33.700
mobility and also to prevent fractures.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.602
Orthopedic research continues to expand to address this and more

00:08:38.602 --> 00:08:41.700
conditions that can affect the skeletal system.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.280
Well, that’s it for the Amoeba Sisters, and we remind you to stay

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:47.700
curious.
